WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.545
ANU SRIVASTAVA: Traditionally,
to write a document,

00:00:02.545 --> 00:00:04.170
you'd start at the
beginning, right?

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.600
But if you're writing
a doc programmatically

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:09.450
with the Google Docs API, did
you know that it's actually

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.220
easier to start at the end?

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.040
Follow along and you'll see
why writing backwards makes

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.320
it easier to perform
batch requests for better

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:17.580
performance.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.020
I'm Anu Srivastava,
and I'm going

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.030
to walk you through this
tip so you can easily

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:24.100
manage your start and end
indexes by making your insert

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.930
text requests in reverse.

00:00:25.930 --> 00:00:27.200
Let's look at an example.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:27.700
Don't worry.

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.100
For the video, we'll
start at the beginning.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.590
I have a collection
of recipes that

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:33.330
need to be moved into Docs.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.240
Moving information from a
database or some other storage

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.360
into a helpful format is
a pretty common issue.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.910
The natural solution is to
read the recipe information

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.660
from my storage and then
write each piece into my doc.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.510
Where it gets complicated
is organizing the updates.

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:50.490
Here, we have a lot
more than just text.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:53.880
We have images, titles,
heading, lists, numbered lists.

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:55.810
It's a lot to consider.

00:00:55.810 --> 00:00:58.170
So let's start with the
overview information.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.680
Here we have a title, an image,
prep time, and then lastly,

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:02.940
yield info.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.519
We'll use Insert Text to
write the title, and then

00:01:05.519 --> 00:01:08.640
Insert Inline Image to add the
photo, and then Insert Text

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.800
again for the yield
and prep time.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:12.900
First, let's look at
one of the requests.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.720
Insert Text is just a note
of what text should go where.

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:18.010
So let's try it out,
starting from the beginning.

00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:20.760
We write our heading, recipe,
and the title of the recipe.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.880
So today, we're making
strawberry vanilla pancakes.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:26.940
It's easy enough to add
the first word at index 1,

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:30.240
but then we need to keep track
of how long that text was so we

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:32.010
can insert that next piece.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.380
So the word "recipe"
is six characters.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.990
I can then insert
the title at index 7.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:38.520
Easy enough, right?

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.760
But the titles will vary in
length from recipe to recipe,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.490
so now we have to keep
track of each of those.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.340
We're going to have to do
this again for every append.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.600
Moving on actually
makes it worse.

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.690
So now I need to
know precisely how

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.850
long my text inserts
were so that I

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.620
know where to insert the photo.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:58.050
And then I have to
remember that an image has

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:01.400
a length of 1 index, no
matter how large the image is.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.370
So back to the documentation.

00:02:03.370 --> 00:02:05.970
This is going to get
real tedious, real fast,

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:09.000
and I'm already worried
about off by one errors.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.780
Imagine keeping track of indexes
for a really large application

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:16.360
with lots of things to insert
coming from different places.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.660
So here is where
our trick comes in.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:21.390
Since you have to set the
start index for every insert,

00:02:21.390 --> 00:02:24.250
what if you started from
the end and always insert it

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:26.160
to the top of the document?

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.520
Our batch update insert
requests will look much cleaner.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.720
I'd use a single
batch update and order

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:33.430
all of the updates in reverse.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:34.830
So start at the bottom.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.230
We'd start with Insert
Text for prep time

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.650
and yield info, then Insert
Inline Image for the recipe

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.030
photo, and then finally, Insert
Text for the title and heading.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.850
It's so much simpler when
everything starts at 1.

00:02:47.850 --> 00:02:50.580
One batch update request
contains all of my edits,

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.380
listed last to first.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.750
Remember, a batch update
is simply a container

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:56.590
for my list of requests.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.400
So now I'm starting at the end
and finishing with a title,

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.530
and no more tracking indexes.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:04.750
Writing backwards, while
counter-intuitive, is simpler.

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:06.900
So you no longer need
to know or even care

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:09.240
about the length of each
one of your inserts.

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:11.063
Ready to try it
out for yourself?

00:03:11.063 --> 00:03:12.480
Check out our
documentation, where

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.272
we have a document
structure guide that you

00:03:14.272 --> 00:03:16.560
might find handy, and let
me know what you think.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:17.880
Happy batching, everyone.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.230
[MUSIC PLAYING]

